\id SNG American King James Version

\ide UTF-8
\h Song of Solomon
\toc1 The Song of Solomon
\toc2 Song of Solomon
\toc3 Sng
\mt1 THE SONG OF SOLOMON
\c 1
\p \v 1 The song of songs, which \add is\add* Solomon's.
\v 2 Let him kiss me with the kisses of his mouth: for your love \add is\add* better than wine.
\v 3 Because of the smell of your good ointments your name \add is as\add* ointment poured forth, therefore do the virgins love you.
\v 4 Draw me, we will run after you: the king has brought me into his chambers: we will be glad and rejoice in you, we will remember your love more than wine: the upright love you.
\v 5 I \add am\add* black, but comely, O you daughters of Jerusalem, as the tents of Kedar, as the curtains of Solomon.
\v 6 Look not on me, because I \add am\add* black, because the sun has looked on me: my mother's children were angry with me; they made me the keeper of the vineyards; \add but\add* my own vineyard have I not kept.
\v 7 Tell me, O you whom my soul loves, where you feed, where you make \add your flock\add* to rest at noon: for why should I be as one that turns aside by the flocks of your companions?
\v 8 If you know not, O you fairest among women, go your way forth by the footsteps of the flock, and feed your kids beside the shepherds' tents.
\v 9 I have compared you, O my love, to a company of horses in Pharaoh's chariots.
\v 10 Your cheeks are comely with rows \add of jewels\add*, your neck with chains \add of gold\add*.
\v 11 We will make you borders of gold with studs of silver.
\v 12 While the king \add sits\add* at his table, my spikenard sends forth the smell thereof.
\v 13 A bundle of myrrh \add is\add* my well-beloved to me; he shall lie all night between my breasts.
\v 14 My beloved \add is\add* to me \add as\add* a cluster of camphire in the vineyards of Engedi.
\v 15 Behold, you \add are\add* fair, my love; behold, you \add are\add* fair; you \add have\add* doves' eyes.
\v 16 Behold, you \add are\add* fair, my beloved, yes, pleasant: also our bed \add is\add* green.
\v 17 The beams of our house \add are\add* cedar, \add and\add* our rafters of fir.
\c 2
\p \v 1 I \add am\add* the rose of Sharon, \add and\add* the lily of the valleys.
\v 2 As the lily among thorns, so \add is\add* my love among the daughters.
\v 3 As the apple tree among the trees of the wood, so \add is\add* my beloved among the sons. I sat down under his shadow with great delight, and his fruit \add was\add* sweet to my taste.
\v 4 He brought me to the banqueting house, and his banner over me \add was\add* love.
\v 5 Stay me with flagons, comfort me with apples: for I \add am\add* sick of love.
\v 6 His left hand \add is\add* under my head, and his right hand does embrace me.
\v 7 I charge you, O you daughters of Jerusalem, by the roes, and by the hinds of the field, that you stir not up, nor awake \add my\add* love, till he please.
\v 8 The voice of my beloved! behold, he comes leaping on the mountains, skipping on the hills.
\v 9 My beloved is like a roe or a young hart: behold, he stands behind our wall, he looks forth at the windows, showing himself through the lattice.
\v 10 My beloved spoke, and said to me, Rise up, my love, my fair one, and come away.
\v 11 For, see, the winter is past, the rain is over \add and\add* gone;
\v 12 The flowers appear on the earth; the time of the singing \add of birds\add* is come, and the voice of the turtle is heard in our land;
\v 13 The fig tree puts forth her green figs, and the vines \add with\add* the tender grape give a \add good\add* smell. Arise, my love, my fair one, and come away.
\v 14 O my dove, \add that are\add* in the clefts of the rock, in the secret \add places\add* of the stairs, let me see your countenance, let me hear your voice; for sweet \add is\add* your voice, and your countenance \add is\add* comely.
\v 15 Take us the foxes, the little foxes, that spoil the vines: for our vines \add have\add* tender grapes.
\v 16 My beloved \add is\add* mine, and I \add am\add* his: he feeds among the lilies.
\v 17 Until the day break, and the shadows flee away, turn, my beloved, and be you like a roe or a young hart on the mountains of Bether.
\c 3
\p \v 1 By night on my bed I sought him whom my soul loves: I sought him, but I found him not.
\v 2 I will rise now, and go about the city in the streets, and in the broad ways I will seek him whom my soul loves: I sought him, but I found him not.
\v 3 The watchmen that go about the city found me: \add to whom I said\add*, Saw you him whom my soul loves?
\v 4 \add It was\add* but a little that I passed from them, but I found him whom my soul loves: I held him, and would not let him go, until I had brought him into my mother's house, and into the chamber of her that conceived me.
\v 5 I charge you, O you daughters of Jerusalem, by the roes, and by the hinds of the field, that you stir not up, nor awake \add my\add* love, till he please.
\v 6 Who \add is\add* this that comes out of the wilderness like pillars of smoke, perfumed with myrrh and frankincense, with all powders of the merchant?
\v 7 Behold his bed, which \add is\add* Solomon's; three score valiant men \add are\add* about it, of the valiant of Israel.
\v 8 They all hold swords, \add being\add* expert in war: every man \add has\add* his sword on his thigh because of fear in the night.
\v 9 King Solomon made himself a chariot of the wood of Lebanon.
\v 10 He made the pillars thereof \add of\add* silver, the bottom thereof \add of\add* gold, the covering of it \add of\add* purple, the middle thereof being paved \add with\add* love, for the daughters of Jerusalem.
\v 11 Go forth, O you daughters of Zion, and behold king Solomon with the crown with which his mother crowned him in the day of his espousals, and in the day of the gladness of his heart.
\c 4
\p \v 1 Behold, you \add are\add* fair, my love; behold, you \add are\add* fair; you \add have\add* doves' eyes within your locks: your hair \add is\add* as a flock of goats, that appear from mount Gilead.
\v 2 Your teeth \add are\add* like a flock \add of sheep that are even\add* shorn, which came up from the washing; whereof every one bear twins, and none \add is\add* barren among them.
\v 3 Your lips \add are\add* like a thread of scarlet, and your speech \add is\add* comely: your temples \add are\add* like a piece of a pomegranate within your locks.
\v 4 Your neck \add is\add* like the tower of David built for an armory, where on there hang a thousand bucklers, all shields of mighty men.
\v 5 Your two breasts \add are\add* like two young roes that are twins, which feed among the lilies.
\v 6 Until the day break, and the shadows flee away, I will get me to the mountain of myrrh, and to the hill of frankincense.
\v 7 You \add are\add* all fair, my love; \add there is\add* no spot in you.
\v 8 Come with me from Lebanon, \add my\add* spouse, with me from Lebanon: look from the top of Amana, from the top of Shenir and Hermon, from the lions' dens, from the mountains of the leopards.
\v 9 You have ravished my heart, my sister, \add my\add* spouse; you have ravished my heart with one of your eyes, with one chain of your neck.
\v 10 How fair is your love, my sister, \add my\add* spouse! how much better is your love than wine! and the smell of your ointments than all spices!
\v 11 Your lips, O \add my\add* spouse, drop \add as\add* the honeycomb: honey and milk \add are\add* under your tongue; and the smell of your garments \add is\add* like the smell of Lebanon.
\v 12 A garden enclosed \add is\add* my sister, \add my\add* spouse; a spring shut up, a fountain sealed.
\v 13 Your plants \add are\add* an orchard of pomegranates, with pleasant fruits; camphire, with spikenard,
\v 14 Spikenard and saffron; calamus and cinnamon, with all trees of frankincense; myrrh and aloes, with all the chief spices:
\v 15 A fountain of gardens, a well of living waters, and streams from Lebanon.
\v 16 Awake, O north wind; and come, you south; blow on my garden, \add that\add* the spices thereof may flow out. Let my beloved come into his garden, and eat his pleasant fruits.
\c 5
\p \v 1 I am come into my garden, my sister, \add my\add* spouse: I have gathered my myrrh with my spice; I have eaten my honeycomb with my honey; I have drunk my wine with my milk: eat, O friends; drink, yes, drink abundantly, O beloved.
\v 2 I sleep, but my heart wakes: \add it is\add* the voice of my beloved that knocks, \add saying\add*, Open to me, my sister, my love, my dove, my undefiled: for my head is filled with dew, \add and\add* my locks with the drops of the night.
\v 3 I have put off my coat; how shall I put it on? I have washed my feet; how shall I defile them?
\v 4 My beloved put in his hand by the hole \add of the door\add*, and my bowels were moved for him.
\v 5 I rose up to open to my beloved; and my hands dropped \add with\add* myrrh, and my fingers \add with\add* sweet smelling myrrh, on the handles of the lock.
\v 6 I opened to my beloved; but my beloved had withdrawn himself, \add and\add* was gone: my soul failed when he spoke: I sought him, but I could not find him; I called him, but he gave me no answer.
\v 7 The watchmen that went about the city found me, they smote me, they wounded me; the keepers of the walls took away my veil from me.
\v 8 I charge you, O daughters of Jerusalem, if you find my beloved, that you tell him, that I \add am\add* sick of love.
\v 9 What \add is\add* your beloved more than \add another\add* beloved, O you fairest among women? what \add is\add* your beloved more than \add another\add* beloved, that you do so charge us?
\v 10 My beloved \add is\add* white and ruddy, the most chief among ten thousand.
\v 11 His head \add is as\add* the most fine gold, his locks \add are\add* bushy, \add and\add* black as a raven.
\v 12 His eyes \add are\add* as \add the eyes\add* of doves by the rivers of waters, washed with milk, \add and\add* fitly set.
\v 13 His cheeks \add are\add* as a bed of spices, \add as\add* sweet flowers: his lips \add like\add* lilies, dropping sweet smelling myrrh.
\v 14 His hands \add are as\add* gold rings set with the beryl: his belly \add is as\add* bright ivory overlaid \add with\add* sapphires.
\v 15 His legs \add are as\add* pillars of marble, set on sockets of fine gold: his countenance \add is\add* as Lebanon, excellent as the cedars.
\v 16 His mouth \add is\add* most sweet: yes, he \add is\add* altogether lovely. This \add is\add* my beloved, and this \add is\add* my friend, O daughters of Jerusalem.
\c 6
\p \v 1 Where is your beloved gone, O you fairest among women? where is your beloved turned aside? that we may seek him with you.
\v 2 My beloved is gone down into his garden, to the beds of spices, to feed in the gardens, and to gather lilies.
\v 3 I \add am\add* my beloved's, and my beloved \add is\add* mine: he feeds among the lilies.
\v 4 You \add are\add* beautiful, O my love, as Tirzah, comely as Jerusalem, terrible as \add an army\add* with banners.
\v 5 Turn away your eyes from me, for they have overcome me: your hair \add is\add* as a flock of goats that appear from Gilead.
\v 6 Your teeth \add are\add* as a flock of sheep which go up from the washing, whereof every one bears twins, and \add there is\add* not one barren among them.
\v 7 As a piece of a pomegranate \add are\add* your temples within your locks.
\v 8 There are three score queens, and fourscore concubines, and virgins without number.
\v 9 My dove, my undefiled is \add but\add* one; she \add is\add* the \add only\add* one of her mother, she \add is\add* the choice \add one\add* of her that bore her. The daughters saw her, and blessed her; \add yes\add*, the queens and the concubines, and they praised her.
\v 10 Who \add is\add* she \add that\add* looks forth as the morning, fair as the moon, clear as the sun, \add and\add* terrible as \add an army\add* with banners?
\v 11 I went down into the garden of nuts to see the fruits of the valley, \add and\add* to see whether the vine flourished \add and\add* the pomegranates budded.
\v 12 Or ever I was aware, my soul made me \add like\add* the chariots of Amminadib.
\v 13 Return, return, O Shulamite; return, return, that we may look on you. What will you see in the Shulamite? As it were the company of two armies.
\c 7
\p \v 1 How beautiful are your feet with shoes, O prince's daughter! the joints of your thighs \add are\add* like jewels, the work of the hands of a cunning workman.
\v 2 Your navel \add is like\add* a round goblet, \add which\add* wants not liquor: your belly \add is like\add* an heap of wheat set about with lilies.
\v 3 Your two breasts \add are\add* like two young roes \add that are\add* twins.
\v 4 Your neck \add is\add* as a tower of ivory; your eyes \add like\add* the fish pools in Heshbon, by the gate of Bathrabbim: your nose \add is\add* as the tower of Lebanon which looks toward Damascus.
\v 5 Your head on you \add is\add* like Carmel, and the hair of your head like purple; the king \add is\add* held in the galleries.
\v 6 How fair and how pleasant are you, O love, for delights!
\v 7 This your stature is like to a palm tree, and your breasts to clusters \add of grapes\add*.
\v 8 I said, I will go up to the palm tree, I will take hold of the boughs thereof: now also your breasts shall be as clusters of the vine, and the smell of your nose like apples;
\v 9 And the roof of your mouth like the best wine for my beloved, that goes \add down\add* sweetly, causing the lips of those that are asleep to speak.
\v 10 I \add am\add* my beloved's, and his desire \add is\add* toward me.
\v 11 Come, my beloved, let us go forth into the field; let us lodge in the villages.
\v 12 Let us get up early to the vineyards; let us see if the vine flourish, \add whether\add* the tender grape appear, \add and\add* the pomegranates bud forth: there will I give you my loves.
\v 13 The mandrakes give a smell, and at our gates \add are\add* all manner of pleasant \add fruits\add*, new and old, \add which\add* I have laid up for you, O my beloved.
\c 8
\p \v 1 O that you \add were\add* as my brother, that sucked the breasts of my mother! \add when\add* I should find you without, I would kiss you; yes, I should not be despised.
\v 2 I would lead you, \add and\add* bring you into my mother's house, \add who\add* would instruct me: I would cause you to drink of spiced wine of the juice of my pomegranate.
\v 3 His left hand \add should be\add* under my head, and his right hand should embrace me.
\v 4 I charge you, O daughters of Jerusalem, that you stir not up, nor awake \add my\add* love, until he please.
\v 5 Who \add is\add* this that comes up from the wilderness, leaning on her beloved? I raised you up under the apple tree: there your mother brought you forth: there she brought you forth \add that\add* bore you.
\v 6 Set me as a seal on your heart, as a seal on your arm: for love \add is\add* strong as death; jealousy \add is\add* cruel as the grave: the coals thereof \add are\add* coals of fire, \add which has a\add* most vehement flame.
\v 7 Many waters cannot quench love, neither can the floods drown it: if \add a\add* man would give all the substance of his house for love, it would utterly be contemned.
\v 8 We have a little sister, and she has no breasts: what shall we do for our sister in the day when she shall be spoken for?
\v 9 If she \add be\add* a wall, we will build on her a palace of silver: and if she \add be\add* a door, we will enclose her with boards of cedar.
\v 10 I \add am\add* a wall, and my breasts like towers: then was I in his eyes as one that found favor.
\v 11 Solomon had a vineyard at Baalhamon; he let out the vineyard to keepers; every one for the fruit thereof was to bring a thousand \add pieces\add* of silver.
\v 12 My vineyard, which \add is\add* mine, \add is\add* before me: you, O Solomon, \add must have\add* a thousand, and those that keep the fruit thereof two hundred.
\v 13 You that dwell in the gardens, the companions listen to your voice: cause me to hear \add it\add*.
\v 14 Make haste, my beloved, and be you like to a roe or to a young hart on the mountains of spices.
